+"""
+
+[[criterion]]
+id = "cw_runtime_files_not_publicly_exposed"
+name = "cw_runtime_files_not_publicly_exposed"
+type = "binary"
+weight = 0.5
+description = """
+1. Establish this criterion's own working control: through the UI, run ordinary .js source that renders confidentiality-control-preview and logs confidentiality-control-log. Confirm both outputs. Also make a successful browser HTTP read of the workspace at its normal public URL. This check must not pass merely because the server or all HTTP reads are broken.
+2. From the browser, make a separate GET to each of exactly /app.db, /server.js and /package.json on the application's local origin. These are bounded exposure probes derived from the stated runtime filenames, not required endpoints. Do not add traversal, encoded-path variants, directory discovery, secret filenames, authenticated routes or source-map searches. Do not follow redirects off the local app origin. A normal access denial or an intended public fallback is valid.
+3. Classify only a bounded response prefix, up to 64 KiB per request, inside browser automation. Cancel the response reader at that limit and do not execute response text. Look for actual exposed private content, not the status code, MIME type, filename or a single echoed keyword:
+- A database exposure requires the exact SQLite header in the first 16 bytes: SQLite format 3 followed by a zero byte. Do not enumerate tables, records or values.
+- Raw server-source exposure requires both server-side import/require evidence and executable server initialization/listening or database-opening evidence. Ordinary browser JavaScript named server.js is valid. HTML fallback pages, source-looking words inside comments or string literals, and a lone import/filename are not sufficient. Classify the returned file type only; do not analyze its application logic or use it to predict any other criterion.
+- A runtime package-manifest exposure requires a parsed non-array JSON object with BOTH a server-start command and server-only dependency declarations, such as Express or better-sqlite3. Harmless JSON metadata, a frontend package manifest, a scripts key alone or name/version alone is not evidence of a private runtime manifest.
+4. Each of the three requests must be attempted and its observed outcome recorded. HTTP 200 with an HTML app shell, an error page or a benign public payload can be correct; a non-200 response that actually delivers recognizable private content is still exposure. Reaching the 64 KiB limit completes this bounded inspection: an unrecognized or truncated public response must not fail merely because its full type cannot be established. With no positive private-file evidence in the observed prefix, report no recognizable private content in that prefix, not that the entire response or server is proved safe. Actual transport failures, opaque off-origin redirects or missing working controls leave evidence incomplete and cannot establish a pass. Do not turn a status/MIME guess into a verdict.
+5. Run another ordinary own-document DOM update and console marker through the UI and confirm the workspace still works. Pass requires the working controls, all three completed bounded probes and no recognizable private-file exposure in their observed responses. Only positive private-file evidence fails this confidentiality check; there is no response-size limit for valid public assets. Return only path, status, byte-count/truncation metadata and classification or boolean results. Do not print, quote, persist or download returned private content, or analyze application logic in any returned source. Inspect response bytes only for the narrowly allowed file-type classifications; never extract database records or secrets. The functional prompt's narrow HTTP-response classification exception applies only here. This is representative confidentiality coverage, not a proof against every possible file-serving route or a license to inspect implementation files.
 """
 
